## What happened

The reading (the deliverable): 93 non-test occurrences of .min( / .max( / .clamp( across src/ (104 raw). That is how many decision sites my mutation instrument structurally cannot ask a question about — and it's a documented design property, not a version gap: cargo-mutants has exactly two genres, FnValue (whole-body replacement) and operator replacement. There is no method-substitution genre, so .min() will never become .max(). No flag fixes it.

All four bets hit — 18 mutants on prompt_retry_limits.rs, none at lines 67/152/160; cost.rs (never read, 350 mutants) names zero of its four min/max sites; and the corollary held: extracting one clamp into `fn inline_retry_ceiling took the list 18 → 19, with a sorted diff showing exactly one added line, an FnValue` body replacement naming the new function. Not an operator mutant that would have existed anyway.

**Three things I made myself write down rather than enjoy:**

1. The remedy is narrower than it sounds. Body replacement asks "does anything notice if this returns nothing?" — never "did you write `.min` where you meant `.max`." Extraction converts no mutant into one coarse mutant. Real, one fn cheap, and not swept repo-wide by this task.
2. **My pre-registered self-check was wrong**, which is worth more than the 4/4. I predicted h0 and h3 would miss, citing six straight rounds where only verbatim-expression bets hit. The pattern died at n=7 — and the discriminator isn't the subject, it's the kind of unknown: bets about what my tests cover keep losing; bets about what the **tool generates** follow from a stateable mechanism.
3. A 4/4 round is a difficulty-calibration warning. Three of four were near-certainties in prediction costume. Only h0 had a real distribution over outcomes.

Readings #1–#4 keep their numbers; a caveat now sits beside them saying each was measured over a population that skips clamp-expressed decisions. Superseded framing recorded, not erased.

